var e=[{topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Absorption is the movement of drug from the site of administration into the systemic circulation in an unchanged form. For an oral tablet the drug must first disintegrate, then dissolve in the gastrointestinal fluid, and only the dissolved molecules can cross the gut wall. Biopharmaceutics is the study of how the formulation and the body together decide how much drug gets in and how fast.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The barrier the drug must cross`},{kind:`para`,text:`The gastrointestinal membrane is a lipid bilayer with protein channels and carriers. It behaves like a sheet of fat: molecules that are lipid soluble and electrically neutral slip through, while charged (ionised) and very water-soluble molecules are held back. Water and very small molecules can pass through aqueous pores.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mechanisms of drug transport`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Carrier`,`Energy (ATP)`,`Saturable`,`Example`],rows:[[`Passive diffusion`,`No`,`No`,`No`,`Most drugs: paracetamol, diazepam`],[`Pore / convective transport`,`No`,`No`,`No`,`Urea, water, ions (MW under 150)`],[`Facilitated diffusion`,`Yes`,`No`,`Yes`,`Vitamin B1, glucose into RBC`],[`Active transport`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Levodopa, methyldopa, 5-fluorouracil`],[`Pinocytosis / endocytosis`,`No`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Oil-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K`]],caption:`Only active transport moves drug against a concentration gradient.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dQ/dt = (D × A × Kp / h) × (Cgi − Cp)`,caption:`Fick's first law of diffusion. dQ/dt = rate of absorption (mg/h); D = diffusion coefficient; A = surface area of absorbing membrane; Kp = partition coefficient of drug between membrane and gut fluid; h = membrane thickness; Cgi = drug concentration in gut fluid; Cp = drug concentration in plasma.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Because Cp is kept very low by distribution and elimination (the sink condition), Cgi − Cp is effectively Cgi. Fick’s law then collapses to a first-order process: rate of absorption is proportional to the amount of drug remaining at the absorption site.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The one-line fact`,text:`The majority of drugs are absorbed by passive diffusion, which is non-saturable, needs no energy and no carrier, and follows first-order kinetics. This single statement has been asked in almost every GPAT paper in some form.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Small intestine is the main absorption site for nearly all drugs because of villi and microvilli (surface area about 200 square metres).`,`Stomach contributes little to absorption despite the favourable pH for weak acids, because its surface area is small and residence time short.`,`Rate-limiting step for a poorly soluble drug is dissolution; for a freely soluble drug it is permeation through the membrane.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Absorption is not bioavailability`,text:`A drug can be 100 percent absorbed from the gut and still have low bioavailability if the liver destroys most of it on first pass (for example propranolol, lignocaine, morphine).`}]},{topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the exam level you must be able to predict, for a given drug and a given pH, how much of it is in the unionised absorbable form, and then list what else can speed the process up or slow it down. That is the pH-partition hypothesis plus the factor lists.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`pH-partition hypothesis`},{kind:`para`,text:`Proposed by Brodie. It states that the gastrointestinal membrane behaves as a lipoidal barrier, drugs are absorbed only in the unionised and lipid-soluble form, and the extent of ionisation is decided by the pKa of the drug and the pH of the fluid at the absorption site.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`For a weak acid:  pH = pKa + log ([ionised] / [unionised])`,caption:`Henderson–Hasselbalch equation for an acid HA. [ionised] = concentration of the salt or conjugate base A−; [unionised] = concentration of free acid HA; pKa = negative log of the acid dissociation constant.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`For a weak base:  pH = pKa + log ([unionised] / [ionised])`,caption:`Henderson–Hasselbalch equation for a base B. [unionised] = free base B; [ionised] = protonated form BH+. Note the numerator and denominator swap compared with an acid.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`% unionised acid = 100 / (1 + 10^(pH − pKa))       % unionised base = 100 / (1 + 10^(pKa − pH))`,caption:`Direct working formulae. The exponent is (pH − pKa) for acids and (pKa − pH) for bases; the result is the fraction available for passive absorption, expressed as a percentage.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug type`,`Stomach pH ~1.5`,`Intestine pH ~6.5`,`Practical outcome`],rows:[[`Weak acid (aspirin, pKa 3.5)`,`Mostly unionised`,`Mostly ionised`,`Absorbed at both sites, mainly intestine`],[`Weak base (quinine, pKa 8.4)`,`Almost fully ionised`,`Partly unionised`,`Absorbed only from intestine`],[`Strong acid (cromoglycate)`,`Ionised`,`Ionised`,`Poorly absorbed at any pH`],[`Strong base (guanethidine)`,`Ionised`,`Ionised`,`Poorly and erratically absorbed`],[`Neutral (chloramphenicol)`,`Unionised`,`Unionised`,`Absorption pH independent`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Factors affecting oral absorption`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Physicochemical: drug solubility and dissolution rate, particle size, salt form, polymorphic form (metastable and amorphous dissolve faster), degree of hydration (anhydrous faster than hydrate), partition coefficient (optimum log P about 2), pKa, complexation, and drug stability in gut fluid.`,`Dosage form: solution is faster than suspension, faster than emulsion, faster than capsule, faster than tablet, faster than coated tablet; disintegrant and lubricant levels, compression force, excipient interactions, wetting agents.`,`Physiological and patient: gastric emptying rate, intestinal transit, gut pH, surface area, blood flow (splanchnic), presystemic metabolism by gut wall and liver, P-glycoprotein efflux, gut flora, age, disease, food and drug interactions.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gastric emptying`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Increases gastric emptying rate`,`Decreases gastric emptying rate`],rows:[[`Large volume of cold water on empty stomach`,`Fatty meal, high-viscosity or hypertonic meal`],[`Mild exercise, lying on the right side`,`Vigorous exercise, lying on the left side`],[`Metoclopramide, domperidone`,`Anticholinergics (atropine, propantheline), opioids`],[`Hunger, anxiety, hyperthyroidism`,`Depression, pain, ulcer, pyloric stenosis, hypothyroidism`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rate-limiting physiology`,text:`Because absorption occurs mainly from the small intestine, gastric emptying is generally the rate-limiting physiological step for the onset of action of an orally administered drug. Rapid emptying is desirable except for drugs degraded in acid or absorbed by a saturable window (riboflavin, iron, levodopa).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Order of release speed`,text:`Solution beats Suspension beats Emulsion beats capSule beats Tablet beats Coated tablet: remember "So Sweet Every Student Takes Chocolate".`}]},{topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced level is about the quantitative framework: the Noyes–Whitney treatment of dissolution, the Biopharmaceutics Classification System that tells you which variable actually limits absorption, and the exceptions where the pH-partition hypothesis fails.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dC/dt = (D × A / h) × (Cs − C)`,caption:`Noyes–Whitney (modified) dissolution rate equation. dC/dt = dissolution rate; D = diffusion coefficient of drug; A = effective surface area of the solid; h = thickness of the stagnant diffusion layer; Cs = saturation solubility; C = concentration in bulk at time t. Under sink conditions C is negligible and the rate is proportional to Cs.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Increase A: micronisation, nanosuspension, solid dispersion. Effective only if the powder is wetted, otherwise micronised hydrophobic powder floats and dissolves slower.`,`Increase Cs: salt formation, metastable polymorph, amorphous form, cosolvency, complexation with cyclodextrin, pH modification in the microenvironment.`,`Decrease h: agitation and gastric motility reduce the diffusion layer thickness.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Biopharmaceutics Classification System (Amidon)`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Solubility`,`Permeability`,`Rate-limiting step`,`Example`],rows:[[`I`,`High`,`High`,`Gastric emptying`,`Metoprolol, paracetamol, propranolol`],[`II`,`Low`,`High`,`Dissolution`,`Ketoconazole, glibenclamide, carbamazepine`],[`III`,`High`,`Low`,`Permeation through membrane`,`Atenolol, cimetidine, ranitidine`],[`IV`,`Low`,`Low`,`Both dissolution and permeation`,`Furosemide, chlorthiazide, taxol`]],caption:`High solubility = highest dose strength soluble in 250 mL of aqueous media over pH 1.2 to 6.8. High permeability = 85 percent or more of the administered dose absorbed.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Biowaiver logic`,text:`A biowaiver (approval on dissolution data instead of a human bioequivalence study) is granted for BCS Class I immediate-release products that dissolve rapidly, and has been extended to Class III drugs with very rapid dissolution. Class II formulation science (micronisation, solid dispersion) gives the biggest bioavailability gain.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Do = (Dose / V0) / Cs        An = Peff × (π × R × L) / Q        Dn = t(residence) / t(dissolution)`,caption:`Dimensionless numbers. Do = dose number (V0 = 250 mL of water taken with the dose, Cs = saturation solubility). An = absorption number (Peff = effective permeability, R and L = radius and length of the intestine, Q = flow). Dn = dissolution number. Absorption is good when Do is low and An and Dn are high.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Where the pH-partition hypothesis breaks down`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Weak acids such as aspirin are absorbed better from the intestine even though they are highly ionised there, because the enormous surface area and long residence time outweigh the lower unionised fraction.`,`Quaternary ammonium compounds (neostigmine) and sulphonic acids are permanently ionised yet show some absorption, probably by ion-pair transport with endogenous counter-ions such as mucin.`,`The virtual membrane pH at the microclimate of the intestinal surface is about 5.3, not the luminal 6.5 to 7.5, so the effective pH gradient differs from the measured one.`,`Carrier-mediated substrates (levodopa, methyldopa, gabapentin, aminopenicillins via PEPT1) ignore pH partition entirely.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Transporters and presystemic loss`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Efflux: P-glycoprotein (MDR1) in the apical enterocyte membrane pumps digoxin, cyclosporine and paclitaxel back into the lumen; inhibited by verapamil, quinidine, ketoconazole and grapefruit juice.`,`Uptake: PEPT1 for beta-lactams and ACE inhibitors, OATP for fexofenadine and statins, LAT1 for levodopa and gabapentin.`,`Gut wall CYP3A4 acts in series with P-gp and can remove a large part of the dose before it reaches the portal vein.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Food effects go both ways`,text:`Food delays absorption of most drugs but increases the extent of absorption of griseofulvin, carbamazepine and lipophilic drugs by stimulating bile secretion; it reduces absorption of tetracyclines (chelation with calcium) and of penicillins and rifampicin (acid degradation with longer gastric residence).`}]},{topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Once a drug reaches the blood it spreads reversibly into tissues. This is distribution. We measure it with one number, the apparent volume of distribution Vd, which links the dose given to the plasma concentration observed.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Vd = Dose (amount of drug in body) / C  =  X0 / C0`,caption:`Apparent volume of distribution. X0 = dose administered intravenously (mg); C0 = plasma concentration at time zero obtained by back-extrapolation (mg/L); Vd is in litres, or L/kg when normalised to body weight.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Vd is called apparent because it is not a real anatomical space. It is the volume that would be needed to contain the whole dose if the drug were present everywhere at the concentration measured in plasma. A drug that hides in fat or muscle gives a low plasma concentration and therefore a huge Vd.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Body fluid compartments in a 70 kg adult`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Compartment`,`Volume`,`L/kg`,`Marker or drug`],rows:[[`Plasma water`,`About 3 L`,`0.04`,`Heparin, warfarin, Evans blue`],[`Extracellular fluid`,`About 14 L`,`0.20`,`Gentamicin, inulin, mannitol`],[`Total body water`,`About 42 L`,`0.60`,`Ethanol, antipyrine, phenytoin`],[`Beyond total body water`,`Over 42 L`,`Over 0.6`,`Digoxin 7 L/kg, chloroquine over 100 L/kg`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Reading a Vd value`,text:`Vd near 3 L means the drug stays in plasma, usually because it is highly bound to plasma albumin. Vd far greater than 42 L means extensive tissue binding or accumulation in fat. A Vd of several hundred litres does not violate physiology because it is only an apparent proportionality constant.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Only unbound (free) drug can leave the capillary, act at the receptor, be metabolised and be filtered by the glomerulus.`,`Plasma protein binding is reversible and follows the law of mass action: drug + protein gives drug–protein complex.`,`Acidic drugs bind mainly to albumin; basic drugs bind mainly to alpha-1-acid glycoprotein.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`High binding is not the same as long action`,text:`Extensive plasma binding restricts the drug to plasma, giving a small Vd and often a long half-life, but the bound fraction is pharmacologically inactive. It is tissue binding, not plasma binding, that produces a large Vd.`}]},{topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The working level covers the three ways Vd is calculated, the proteins involved in binding, the free fraction concept, and the displacement interactions that are the classic examination material.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Calculating the volume of distribution`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Vd (extrapolated) = X0 / C0        Vd (area) = X0 / (Kel × AUC)        Vd = Cl / Kel`,caption:`X0 = intravenous dose; C0 = extrapolated zero-time plasma concentration; Kel = first-order elimination rate constant (per hour); AUC = area under the plasma concentration–time curve from 0 to infinity; Cl = total body clearance (L/h). Vd(area) is the value normally quoted for multi-compartment drugs.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Loading dose = Vd × Css (target)  /  F        Maintenance dose rate = Cl × Css / F`,caption:`Vd = volume of distribution (L); Css = target steady-state plasma concentration (mg/L); F = bioavailable fraction; Cl = clearance (L/h). Loading dose depends only on Vd, maintenance dose only on clearance.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Factors affecting distribution`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Drug factors: lipid solubility, molecular size, pKa and degree of ionisation, plasma and tissue protein binding, affinity for specific tissues.`,`Physiological: tissue perfusion (heart, liver, kidney, brain get drug first; fat and skin later), membrane permeability barriers such as the blood–brain and placental barrier, body composition (fat percentage rises with age and in obesity).`,`Pathological: hypoalbuminaemia in nephrotic syndrome, burns, cirrhosis and malnutrition raises free drug; oedema and ascites raise the Vd of hydrophilic drugs; uraemia displaces acidic drugs from albumin.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Plasma protein binding`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Protein`,`Normal level`,`Binds`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Albumin`,`3.5 to 5.0 g/dL`,`Acidic and neutral drugs`,`Warfarin, phenytoin, salicylates, sulphonamides`],[`Alpha-1-acid glycoprotein`,`0.04 to 0.1 g/dL`,`Basic drugs`,`Lignocaine, propranolol, imipramine, quinidine`],[`Lipoproteins`,`Variable`,`Very lipophilic drugs`,`Cyclosporine, amphotericin B`],[`Globulins and transcortin`,`Variable`,`Steroids, vitamins, metals`,`Corticosteroids, thyroxine, iron`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`fu = Cu / C(total)        % bound = 100 × (C − Cu) / C`,caption:`fu = fraction unbound (free); Cu = unbound plasma concentration; C = total plasma concentration. Only Cu is available for pharmacological effect, glomerular filtration and hepatic metabolism.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The 95 to 90 rule`,text:`For a drug that is 95 percent bound, free fraction is 5 percent. If displacement reduces binding to 90 percent, the free fraction is 10 percent, that is the pharmacologically active concentration has doubled after only a 5 percent change in binding. This calculation is asked repeatedly.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Displaced drug`,`Displacer`,`Clinical result`],rows:[[`Warfarin`,`Phenylbutazone, aspirin, sulphonamides`,`Bleeding, raised INR`],[`Bilirubin (in the neonate)`,`Sulphonamides, vitamin K analogues`,`Kernicterus`],[`Methotrexate`,`Salicylates, sulphonamides, probenecid`,`Bone marrow suppression`],[`Tolbutamide`,`Sulphonamides, salicylates`,`Hypoglycaemia`],[`Phenytoin`,`Valproate, salicylates`,`Toxicity at a normal total level`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Displacement is usually transient`,text:`A displacement interaction is clinically important only when the drug has a narrow therapeutic index, high binding (over 90 percent), small Vd and a low extraction ratio. For most drugs the extra free drug is simply cleared faster, so total concentration falls and free concentration returns to normal.`}]},{topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the advanced level Vd is treated as a derived physiological quantity rather than a fitted constant, and protein binding is analysed with binding equations and Scatchard plots.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Vd = Vp + Vt × (fu / fu,t)`,caption:`Physiological (Oie–Tozer type) expression. Vp = plasma volume (about 3 L); Vt = volume of tissue water outside plasma (about 39 L); fu = fraction unbound in plasma; fu,t = fraction unbound in tissue. Vd rises when plasma binding falls (fu up) and falls when tissue binding falls (fu,t up).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Digoxin has a Vd of about 500 L because fu,t is tiny: it binds hard to Na+/K+-ATPase in cardiac and skeletal muscle.`,`Quinidine displaces digoxin from tissue binding sites, so digoxin Vd falls, plasma concentration rises and toxicity follows: this is a tissue displacement, not a plasma displacement.`,`In hypoalbuminaemia the total plasma phenytoin level falls while the free level (the active one) is unchanged, so dosing on a total level alone leads to over-treatment.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quantifying binding`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`r = (n × Ka × [Df]) / (1 + Ka × [Df])       and       r / [Df] = n × Ka − r × Ka`,caption:`Langmuir binding isotherm and its Scatchard rearrangement. r = moles of drug bound per mole of protein; n = number of binding sites per protein molecule; Ka = association constant (per molar); [Df] = free drug concentration. A Scatchard plot of r/[Df] against r is linear for one class of sites, slope = −Ka and x-intercept = n.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Albumin site I (warfarin site) binds bulky heterocyclic anions: warfarin, phenylbutazone, phenytoin, valproate.`,`Albumin site II (diazepam or indole–benzodiazepine site) binds aromatic carboxylic acids: diazepam, ibuprofen, naproxen, semisynthetic penicillins.`,`Methods of measuring binding: equilibrium dialysis (reference method), ultrafiltration, ultracentrifugation, gel filtration, spectroscopic and calorimetric methods.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Alpha-1-acid glycoprotein is an acute-phase reactant`,text:`Its concentration rises in myocardial infarction, surgery, trauma, inflammation, Crohn disease and malignancy, and falls in nephrotic syndrome and in the neonate. Total lignocaine or propranolol levels therefore rise after infarction while free drug stays constant.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Barrier`,`Structure`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`Blood–brain barrier`,`Tight junctions, no fenestration, P-gp efflux`,`Only unionised lipophilic drugs enter`],[`Placental barrier`,`Lipoidal, simple diffusion`,`Most drugs cross; ion trapping in the more acidic fetus`],[`Blood–testis barrier`,`Sertoli cell tight junctions`,`Poor penetration of most antimicrobials`],[`Blood–CSF barrier`,`Choroid plexus epithelium`,`Inflammation increases penicillin entry`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ion trapping in milk and fetus`,text:`Breast milk (pH about 6.6) and the fetal compartment are slightly more acidic than maternal plasma (pH 7.4). Basic drugs become protonated and are trapped there, so basic drugs concentrate in milk while acidic drugs do not.`}]},{topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Elimination is the irreversible loss of drug from the body, and it has two arms: metabolism (chemical change, mainly in the liver) and excretion (physical removal, mainly by the kidney). Clearance is the single number that measures how efficiently the body does this.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Clearance = Rate of elimination / Plasma concentration        Cl (total) = Cl(hepatic) + Cl(renal) + Cl(other)`,caption:`Clearance is the volume of plasma completely cleared of drug per unit time (L/h or mL/min). Rate of elimination is in mg/h and plasma concentration in mg/L. Clearances from parallel organs are additive.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Metabolism: two phases`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phase`,`Reactions`,`Enzyme`,`Product`],rows:[[`Phase I (functionalisation)`,`Oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis`,`Cytochrome P450, esterases`,`More polar, may still be active`],[`Phase II (conjugation)`,`Glucuronidation, sulphation, acetylation, glutathione, methylation, glycine`,`UGT, SULT, NAT, GST`,`Usually inactive, water soluble, excreted`]],caption:`Glucuronidation is the commonest phase II route in humans. Phase II conjugates are almost always inactive, the classic exception being morphine-6-glucuronide, which is more active than morphine.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Liver is the principal site; gut wall, lung, kidney, plasma and skin also metabolise drugs.`,`CYP3A4 handles roughly half of all marketed drugs; CYP2D6 shows genetic polymorphism (poor and ultrarapid metabolisers).`,`Enzyme inducers (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone, smoking, chronic alcohol) lower plasma levels; inhibitors (ketoconazole, erythromycin, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, grapefruit juice) raise them.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Renal excretion: three processes`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Glomerular filtration: passive, only unbound drug of molecular weight below about 60000 is filtered; normal glomerular filtration rate is about 120 mL/min.`,`Active tubular secretion: carrier-mediated, occurs in the proximal tubule by separate acid and base transporters, saturable and subject to competition (probenecid blocks penicillin secretion).`,`Tubular reabsorption: mostly passive, favours unionised lipid-soluble drug, and is therefore strongly pH dependent, which is the basis of forced alkaline or acid diuresis in poisoning.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The excretion equation to memorise`,text:`Rate of excretion = rate of filtration + rate of secretion − rate of reabsorption. Renal clearance greater than the filtration clearance (fu × GFR) proves net secretion; renal clearance less than it proves net reabsorption.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Clearance and half-life are not the same thing`,text:`Half-life depends on both clearance and volume of distribution. A patient can have unchanged clearance and a longer half-life purely because Vd increased (for example oedema), so half-life alone is a poor index of eliminating capacity.`}]},{topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The working level is built on hepatic extraction ratio, the well-stirred model, first-pass metabolism and the arithmetic of renal clearance and creatinine clearance.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hepatic clearance and extraction ratio`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`ER = (Ca − Cv) / Ca        Cl(hepatic) = Q × ER        F(max) = 1 − ER`,caption:`ER = hepatic extraction ratio (0 to 1); Ca = drug concentration in blood entering the liver (portal plus arterial); Cv = concentration leaving in the hepatic vein; Q = hepatic blood flow, about 1.5 L/min or 90 L/h in an adult; F(max) = maximum oral bioavailability possible when absorption is complete.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cl(hepatic) = Q × (fu × Cl(int)) / (Q + fu × Cl(int))`,caption:`Well-stirred (venous equilibrium) model. Q = hepatic blood flow; fu = fraction unbound in blood; Cl(int) = intrinsic clearance, the maximum ability of the enzymes to metabolise drug in the absence of flow and binding limitations.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`High extraction (ER over 0.7)`,`Low extraction (ER under 0.3)`],rows:[[`Limited by`,`Hepatic blood flow`,`Intrinsic clearance and free fraction`],[`Effect of enzyme induction`,`Little on clearance, large on oral F`,`Large fall in plasma levels`],[`Effect of protein binding change`,`Minimal`,`Marked (restrictive clearance)`],[`Effect of shock or propranolol`,`Clearance falls with flow`,`Little effect`],[`Examples`,`Propranolol, lignocaine, morphine, verapamil, nitroglycerin, imipramine`,`Warfarin, phenytoin, diazepam, theophylline, tolbutamide`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`First-pass effect`,text:`A drug with a high extraction ratio undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism, so oral bioavailability is low and variable and the oral dose is far larger than the parenteral dose (propranolol 40 mg oral versus 1 mg intravenous). Routes that bypass the portal circulation are sublingual, rectal (lower two-thirds), transdermal, inhalational and parenteral.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Renal clearance`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cl(renal) = (Cu × Vu) / Cp = Rate of urinary excretion / Plasma concentration`,caption:`Cu = drug concentration in urine; Vu = urine flow rate (mL/min); Cp = plasma concentration at the mid-point of the collection; Cu × Vu = rate of urinary excretion (mg/min).`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Renal clearance value`,`Interpretation`,`Marker or drug`],rows:[[`Equal to fu × GFR`,`Filtration only`,`Inulin, creatinine (approximately)`],[`Less than fu × GFR`,`Net tubular reabsorption`,`Glucose, diazepam, ethanol`],[`Greater than fu × GFR`,`Net tubular secretion`,`Penicillin, PAH, frusemide`],[`About 650 mL/min`,`Complete extraction each pass`,`Para-aminohippuric acid (measures renal plasma flow)`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`CrCl (mL/min) = [(140 − age in years) × body weight in kg] / (72 × serum creatinine in mg/dL)      × 0.85 for females`,caption:`Cockcroft–Gault equation for creatinine clearance, used as a surrogate for glomerular filtration rate when adjusting doses of renally cleared drugs such as aminoglycosides, digoxin and vancomycin.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Alkalinising urine with sodium bicarbonate ionises weak acids (aspirin, phenobarbitone), preventing reabsorption and speeding excretion.`,`Acidifying urine with ammonium chloride ionises weak bases (amphetamine, quinine) and speeds their excretion.`,`Enterohepatic recycling: drug is conjugated in the liver, excreted in bile, hydrolysed by gut beta-glucuronidase and reabsorbed, which prolongs half-life (oral contraceptives, morphine, digitoxin, rifampicin, phenolphthalein). Broad-spectrum antibiotics interrupt this cycle and can cause contraceptive failure.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Enzyme inducers`,text:`"CRAP GPS": Carbamazepine, Rifampicin, Alcohol (chronic), Phenytoin, Griseofulvin, Phenobarbitone, Sulphonylureas and Smoking.`}]},{topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test whether you can reason about what happens to clearance, half-life and steady-state concentration when binding, blood flow or enzyme activity changes independently.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Change`,`Low ER drug`,`High ER drug`],rows:[[`fu doubles (displacement)`,`Cl doubles, Css(total) halves, Css(free) unchanged`,`Cl unchanged, Vd rises, free level rises`],[`Enzyme induction`,`Cl rises, oral F unchanged`,`Cl rises slightly, oral F falls sharply`],[`Liver blood flow falls`,`No real change`,`Cl falls in proportion to flow`],[`Cirrhosis`,`Moderate fall in Cl`,`Large rise in oral bioavailability`]],caption:`Restrictive clearance (low ER) is sensitive to binding; non-restrictive clearance (high ER) is not.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`AUC(oral) = F × Dose / Cl = (Fa × Fg × Fh × Dose) / (fu × Cl(int))`,caption:`For a low extraction drug given orally the fu terms cancel, so oral AUC depends only on intrinsic clearance. Fa = fraction absorbed; Fg = fraction escaping gut wall metabolism; Fh = fraction escaping hepatic first pass; fu = unbound fraction; Cl(int) = intrinsic clearance.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The classic reasoning question`,text:`For an orally administered low-extraction drug, a displacement interaction raises the free fraction but does not change the average free steady-state concentration, because clearance of unbound drug (Cl(int)) is unchanged. Total measured concentration falls, which is why therapeutic drug monitoring of phenytoin in uraemia must use free levels.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Special metabolic points`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Paracetamol at therapeutic dose is glucuronidated and sulphated; in overdose these saturate and CYP2E1 forms NAPQI, which depletes glutathione and causes hepatic necrosis. N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione.`,`Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide, dapsone and sulphamethazine show acetylation polymorphism: slow acetylators risk peripheral neuropathy and lupus-like syndrome.`,`Prodrugs needing activation: enalapril to enalaprilat, levodopa to dopamine, cyclophosphamide, sulphasalazine (by colonic flora), aspirin to salicylic acid.`,`Zero-order (capacity-limited) metabolism at therapeutic doses: phenytoin, ethanol, salicylate at high dose, theophylline in some patients.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cl (from urine data) = Ae(infinity) × Kel / AUC        fe = Ae(infinity) / (F × Dose)`,caption:`Ae(infinity) = total amount of unchanged drug recovered in urine; Kel = elimination rate constant; AUC = area under the plasma curve; fe = fraction of the absorbed dose excreted unchanged. Renal clearance = fe × total clearance.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Biliary excretion thresholds`,text:`Biliary excretion favours molecules with molecular weight above about 500 (in humans 300 to 500 is borderline), polar anions and cations and conjugates. Below about 300 the compound is reabsorbed and returns to plasma, so it appears in urine instead.`}]},{topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A compartment model is a mathematical simplification: we pretend the body is one or two well-stirred tanks so that plasma concentrations can be described by simple exponential equations. The one-compartment open model with intravenous bolus is the starting point of every pharmacokinetic calculation.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`C = C0 × e^(−Kel × t)        log C = log C0 − (Kel × t) / 2.303`,caption:`One-compartment intravenous bolus. C = plasma concentration at time t; C0 = concentration at time zero (extrapolated); Kel = first-order elimination rate constant (per hour); t = time. A plot of log C against t is a straight line of slope −Kel/2.303.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`t½ = 0.693 / Kel        Kel = 0.693 / t½`,caption:`Biological half-life for a first-order process. t½ = time for the plasma concentration to fall by half (hours); 0.693 = natural logarithm of 2. Half-life is independent of dose for first-order kinetics.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cl = Kel × Vd        AUC(0 to infinity) = C0 / Kel = Dose / Cl`,caption:`Cl = total body clearance (L/h); Kel = elimination rate constant (per hour); Vd = apparent volume of distribution (L); AUC = area under the plasma concentration–time curve (mg·h/L).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Zero order versus first order`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`First order`,`Zero order`],rows:[[`Rate equation`,`dC/dt = −Kel × C`,`dC/dt = −K0`],[`Integrated form`,`C = C0 e^(−Kel t)`,`C = C0 − K0 t`],[`Linear plot`,`log C versus t`,`C versus t`],[`Half-life`,`Constant, 0.693/Kel`,`Varies: C0 / (2 K0)`],[`Units of rate constant`,`per hour`,`mg per hour`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The four to five half-life rule`,text:`After 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 half-lives, 50, 75, 87.5, 93.75 and 96.9 percent of the process is complete. So a drug is essentially eliminated, or a constant-rate infusion has essentially reached steady state, in about 4 to 5 half-lives, regardless of the dose or infusion rate.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Kel is not clearance`,text:`Kel is a fractional rate (the proportion of the body content removed per hour) and depends on Vd; clearance is a volume per hour and is the true measure of eliminating organ efficiency.`}]},{topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam-level content is the set of equations for intravenous infusion and for extravascular (oral) administration, and the graphical techniques used to extract rate constants from data.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Constant-rate intravenous infusion`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`C = (R0 / Cl) × (1 − e^(−Kel × t))        Css = R0 / Cl = R0 / (Kel × Vd)`,caption:`R0 = infusion rate (mg/h); Cl = clearance (L/h); Kel = elimination rate constant; Vd = volume of distribution; Css = steady-state plasma concentration reached when input rate equals output rate. Time to steady state depends only on t½, never on R0.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Loading dose = Css × Vd        and with an infusion running, LD = Css × Vd (given as a bolus)`,caption:`A loading dose fills the volume of distribution immediately so the target concentration is reached at once; the infusion then maintains it. Css = desired steady-state concentration; Vd = apparent volume of distribution.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Extravascular administration`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`C = [(F × X0 × Ka) / (Vd × (Ka − Kel))] × (e^(−Kel × t) − e^(−Ka × t))`,caption:`Bateman equation for first-order absorption into a one-compartment model. F = bioavailable fraction; X0 = dose; Ka = first-order absorption rate constant (per hour); Kel = elimination rate constant; Vd = apparent volume of distribution.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`tmax = 2.303 × log (Ka / Kel) / (Ka − Kel)        Cmax = (F × X0 / Vd) × e^(−Kel × tmax)`,caption:`tmax = time of peak concentration; it depends only on the rate constants, not on the dose. Cmax = peak plasma concentration and is proportional to F and to dose. Increasing Ka lowers tmax and raises Cmax but does not change AUC.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`What each parameter tells you`,text:`Cmax and tmax describe the rate of absorption; AUC describes the extent of absorption. A formulation change that alters Cmax and tmax but leaves AUC unchanged has altered rate only, and the two products remain equally bioavailable in extent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Graphical methods`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Method of residuals (feathering, curve stripping): plot log C against t, take the terminal linear phase and extrapolate it back; subtract the observed points from the extrapolated line during the early phase; the residual line has slope −Ka/2.303 for oral data or −alpha/2.303 for a two-compartment intravenous curve.`,`Wagner–Nelson method: applicable to a one-compartment model, needs only plasma data, gives the cumulative fraction of drug absorbed and hence Ka; the percentage remaining to be absorbed plotted on log scale against time gives a straight line of slope −Ka/2.303.`,`Loo–Riegelman method: the two-compartment equivalent of Wagner–Nelson, and it requires intravenous data in the same subject.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Wagner–Nelson:  (Xa / V)t = Ct + Kel × AUC(0 to t)        Fraction absorbed = [Ct + Kel × AUC(0−t)] / [Kel × AUC(0−infinity)]`,caption:`Xa = cumulative amount absorbed up to time t; V = volume of distribution; Ct = plasma concentration at time t; Kel = elimination rate constant; AUC(0−t) = area up to time t; AUC(0−infinity) = total area.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Trapezoidal rule:  AUC(t1 to t2) = (C1 + C2) × (t2 − t1) / 2        AUC(tlast to infinity) = Clast / Kel`,caption:`C1 and C2 = plasma concentrations at consecutive times t1 and t2. Total AUC = sum of all trapezoids plus the extrapolated tail Clast/Kel, where Clast is the last measurable concentration.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Flip-flop kinetics`,text:`Normally Ka is much larger than Kel and the terminal slope gives Kel. If absorption is slower than elimination (Ka less than Kel), as in a sustained-release or depot product, the terminal slope actually gives Ka and the residual line gives Kel. The two constants have swapped roles.`}]},{topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The two-compartment model and the non-compartmental (moment) approach are the advanced material, along with urinary excretion methods for estimating rate constants.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`C = A × e^(−alpha × t) + B × e^(−beta × t)`,caption:`Biexponential decline for a two-compartment open model given intravenously. A and B = zero-time intercepts of the distribution and elimination phases; alpha = hybrid distribution (fast) rate constant; beta = hybrid elimination (slow, terminal) rate constant, so t½(beta) = 0.693/beta. C0 = A + B.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`alpha × beta = K10 × K21        alpha + beta = K12 + K21 + K10        K21 = (A×beta + B×alpha)/(A + B)`,caption:`Relations between hybrid and micro rate constants. K12 = transfer from central to peripheral compartment; K21 = peripheral to central; K10 = elimination from the central compartment. Vc = Dose / (A + B) is the central compartment volume.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The distribution (alpha) phase is steep and short; the elimination (beta) phase is shallow and long and is the one used for dosing decisions.`,`Loading doses calculated from Vd(area) can transiently overshoot in the central compartment: this is why intravenous phenytoin and lignocaine loading must be given slowly or in divided boluses.`,`Vd(ss) = Vc + Vp is the most physiologically meaningful volume and is independent of clearance; Vd(area) = Cl/beta changes when clearance changes.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Non-compartmental analysis`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`MRT = AUMC / AUC        Vd(ss) = Dose × AUMC / (AUC)^2        Cl = Dose / AUC        MRT = 1 / Kel (one compartment, IV bolus)`,caption:`AUMC = area under the first-moment curve (plot of concentration × time against time); AUC = area under the curve; MRT = mean residence time, the average time a molecule stays in the body. For oral dosing, MAT = MRT(oral) − MRT(iv) = 1/Ka.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rate method:  log (dXu/dt) = log (Ke × X0) − Kel × t / 2.303        Sigma-minus:  log (Xu(infinity) − Xu) = log Xu(infinity) − Kel × t / 2.303`,caption:`Urinary excretion methods. dXu/dt = rate of urinary excretion at the mid-point of the collection interval; Ke = renal excretion rate constant; X0 = dose; Xu = cumulative amount excreted up to time t; Xu(infinity) = total amount finally excreted. Both plots give a slope of −Kel/2.303.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rate versus sigma-minus`,text:`The sigma-minus (amount remaining to be excreted) method is more accurate because it is less sensitive to fluctuation in urine flow, but it demands urine collection until practically all the drug is out, that is at least 7 half-lives. The rate method needs a shorter collection but scatters more.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which half-life is quoted?`,text:`For a two-compartment drug the half-life reported in textbooks is the terminal (beta) half-life, not 0.693/K10. Using K10 will overestimate how quickly the drug leaves the body because it ignores the drug returning from the peripheral compartment.`}]},{topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Bioavailability (F) is the rate and extent to which the active drug reaches the systemic circulation from its dosage form. By definition an intravenous dose is 100 percent bioavailable, so every other route is compared with it.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Absolute bioavailability, F = [AUC(oral) / AUC(iv)] × [Dose(iv) / Dose(oral)]`,caption:`AUC = area under the plasma concentration–time curve from zero to infinity; Dose(iv) and Dose(oral) are the doses actually given by the two routes. F has no units and lies between 0 and 1 (or 0 to 100 percent).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Relative bioavailability, Fr = [AUC(test) / AUC(standard)] × [Dose(standard) / Dose(test)]`,caption:`Used when no intravenous form exists. The standard is a marketed reference product or an oral solution. Relative bioavailability can exceed 100 percent if the test product is better absorbed than the reference.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three measured parameters`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`What it measures`,`Units`],rows:[[`Cmax`,`Rate of absorption; must exceed MEC for effect`,`mg/L or µg/mL`],[`tmax`,`Rate of absorption; time of the peak`,`hours`],[`AUC`,`Extent of absorption; total drug reaching circulation`,`mg·h/L`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Pharmaceutical equivalents: same active drug, same dose, same dosage form, same route, meeting the same compendial standards.`,`Bioequivalent products: pharmaceutical equivalents whose rate and extent of absorption are not statistically different under a suitable study design.`,`Therapeutic equivalents: bioequivalent products expected to give the same clinical effect and safety profile.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why bioavailability can be low`,text:`Incomplete release from the dosage form, poor aqueous solubility, degradation in gastric acid or by gut enzymes, complexation with food, efflux by P-glycoprotein, gut wall metabolism, and hepatic first-pass metabolism. Only the last of these is unrelated to how much drug crossed the gut wall.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Urinary data can substitute for plasma data`,text:`When the drug is largely excreted unchanged, bioavailability can be measured from cumulative urinary excretion Xu(infinity) instead of AUC, using the same ratio formula. The peak excretion rate replaces Cmax.`}]},{topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The regulatory core of the topic is the bioequivalence study: how it is designed, what is measured, how the statistics are done and what limits must be met.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Study design`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Standard design: single-dose, two-treatment, two-period, two-sequence, randomised, open-label crossover in 18 to 36 healthy adult volunteers, fasted overnight.`,`Washout period of at least 5 elimination half-lives (usually 7 days or more) separates the periods so no drug carries over.`,`Crossover is preferred because each subject is his or her own control, which removes between-subject variability, the largest source of noise.`,`Parallel design is used when the half-life is very long (over 24 hours) or the drug is toxic; replicate crossover is used for highly variable drugs (within-subject CV over 30 percent).`,`Steady-state (multiple-dose) studies are used for modified-release products and for drugs with non-linear kinetics.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Statistical acceptance`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`90 percent confidence interval of the test / reference ratio of log-transformed AUC and Cmax must lie within 80.00 to 125.00 percent`,caption:`The two one-sided tests procedure at alpha = 0.05. AUC and Cmax are log-transformed because they are log-normally distributed; the limits are asymmetric (80 to 125) because 0.80 and 1.25 are reciprocals on the log scale. tmax is compared non-parametrically and only where rapid onset matters.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Situation`,`Acceptance range for AUC`,`Note`],rows:[[`Ordinary drug`,`80.00 to 125.00 percent`,`90 percent CI, log-transformed`],[`Narrow therapeutic index drug`,`90.00 to 111.11 percent`,`Warfarin, digoxin, lithium, phenytoin`],[`Highly variable drug`,`Scaled (widened) limits`,`Reference-scaled average bioequivalence`],[`Cmax (general)`,`80.00 to 125.00 percent`,`May be widened for some products`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The number to remember`,text:`Bioequivalence is accepted when the 90 percent confidence interval for the geometric mean ratio of the log-transformed AUC and Cmax falls entirely within 80 to 125 percent. Not 95 percent CI, and not 80 to 120 percent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`In vitro in vivo correlation (IVIVC)`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Level`,`What is correlated`,`Value`],rows:[[`A`,`Point-to-point: in vitro dissolution curve versus in vivo absorption curve`,`Highest; supports a biowaiver`],[`B`,`Statistical moments: mean dissolution time versus mean residence time`,`Not point-to-point, weakest predictive use`],[`C`,`Single point: one dissolution point versus one PK parameter`,`Useful early in development`],[`Multiple C`,`Several dissolution points versus one or more PK parameters`,`Approaches level A in usefulness`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Bioavailability study is required for a new drug, a new dosage form or route, a new formulation of an existing drug, and where a documented bioavailability problem exists.`,`A bioequivalence study is required for generics of drugs where the classic list applies: narrow therapeutic index, poor solubility, non-linear kinetics, and documented absorption problems.`,`Blood sampling should continue for at least 3 elimination half-lives, and the truncated AUC(0−t) should cover at least 80 percent of AUC(0−infinity).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Superimposable curves are not required`,text:`Two products can differ visibly in tmax and still be bioequivalent, provided the confidence intervals for AUC and Cmax pass. Conversely, equal AUC alone is not enough: Cmax must also fall within limits, because rate matters for single-dose therapy.`}]},{topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced level concerns the analysis of variance behind the confidence interval, the exceptions to the crossover design, biowaiver policy and the specific traps in modified-release bioequivalence.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The crossover ANOVA partitions variability into sequence, subject within sequence, period and treatment effects. A significant sequence effect signals inadequate washout (true carry-over) and invalidates the crossover analysis.`,`Sample size depends on the intra-subject coefficient of variation, the expected test/reference ratio and the power required (usually 80 to 90 percent). Doubling the CV roughly quadruples the number of subjects.`,`Studies are usually done in the fasted state; a fed study is additionally required for modified-release products and where the label recommends food, because of dose dumping risk.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Dose dumping risk assessed as:  Cmax(fed) / Cmax(fasted)  and by  f2 similarity factor = 50 × log {[1 + (1/n) Σ (Rt − Tt)^2]^(−0.5) × 100}`,caption:`f2 = similarity factor for dissolution profiles; n = number of time points; Rt and Tt = percentage dissolved of reference and test at time t. f2 between 50 and 100 means the profiles are similar; f1 (difference factor) should be 0 to 15.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`f2 conditions`,text:`The f2 test is valid only when at least 3 to 4 time points are used, not more than one point beyond 85 percent dissolution is included, and the coefficient of variation is under 20 percent at the earliest point and under 10 percent later.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Endpoint`,`Use`],rows:[[`AUC(0−t) and AUC(0−infinity)`,`Extent of absorption, primary endpoint`],[`Cmax`,`Rate of absorption, co-primary endpoint`],[`partial AUC (pAUC)`,`Rate-sensitive products, e.g. methylphenidate, zolpidem`],[`Cmin and fluctuation`,`Steady-state and modified-release studies`],[`Cmax/AUC ratio`,`A more sensitive index of absorption rate than Cmax alone`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Biowaivers are granted on the basis of BCS class (Class I and rapidly dissolving Class III), for different strengths of a proportionally similar formulation, and for oral solutions containing no excipient that alters absorption.`,`Pharmacodynamic and clinical endpoint studies replace plasma measurement when the drug is not absorbed systemically (topical corticosteroids by vasoconstrictor assay, inhaled bronchodilators by FEV1, antifungal creams by clinical cure).`,`Enantiomers should be measured separately when the two isomers differ in activity and their exposure ratio changes with formulation.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Bioavailability is about the unchanged drug`,text:`The parent drug is measured, not the metabolite, unless the parent is unmeasurable or the metabolite carries the activity (as with a prodrug). Measuring the metabolite blunts differences in absorption rate because the metabolite curve is smoothed by the conversion step.`}]},{topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Linear (dose-independent) pharmacokinetics means that doubling the dose doubles the plasma concentration and the AUC, while half-life and clearance stay constant. When a carrier or enzyme saturates, this proportionality breaks down and the kinetics become non-linear or dose-dependent.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`−dC/dt = (Vmax × C) / (Km + C)`,caption:`Michaelis–Menten equation applied to elimination. Vmax = maximum rate of metabolism (mg/day or mg/h); Km = Michaelis constant, the drug concentration at which the rate is half of Vmax (mg/L); C = plasma concentration.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`When C is much smaller than Km, the equation reduces to (Vmax/Km) × C, which is first-order: Vmax/Km behaves as the intrinsic clearance.`,`When C is much larger than Km, the rate becomes Vmax, a constant: this is zero-order or saturation kinetics.`,`Drugs showing capacity-limited kinetics in the therapeutic range: phenytoin, ethanol, salicylate (high dose), theophylline, and dicoumarol.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Signature of non-linearity`,text:`A more than proportional rise in AUC and steady-state concentration with dose, a half-life that lengthens as the dose rises, and a clearance that falls with dose. Ethanol is the classic pure zero-order example, eliminated at about 10 mL (or 10 g) per hour irrespective of the amount drunk.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Multiple dosing: the idea of accumulation`},{kind:`para`,text:`If a repeat dose is given before the previous one has been eliminated, drug accumulates. Accumulation continues until the amount lost in one dosing interval equals the amount given, which is the steady state. It takes about 4 to 5 half-lives, no matter what the dose or interval is.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Accumulation ratio, R = 1 / (1 − e^(−Kel × tau))`,caption:`R = ratio of steady-state concentration to first-dose concentration at the same time point; Kel = elimination rate constant; tau = dosing interval. When tau equals one half-life, e^(−Kel×tau) = 0.5 and R = 2.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Changing the dose does not change the time to steady state`,text:`A bigger dose gives a higher steady-state level but reaches it in the same 4 to 5 half-lives. Only a loading dose gets you there faster, and only a change in half-life changes the time required.`}]},{topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The applied level is the arithmetic of a multiple-dose regimen: average steady-state concentration, peaks and troughs, fluctuation, and how a dosing interval is chosen; plus the practical handling of phenytoin.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Css(average) = (F × X0) / (Cl × tau) = (F × X0) / (Kel × Vd × tau) = 1.44 × t½ × F × X0 / (Vd × tau)`,caption:`Css(average) = average plasma concentration at steady state over one interval; F = bioavailable fraction; X0 = maintenance dose; Cl = clearance; tau = dosing interval; Vd = volume of distribution; 1.44 = 1/0.693.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Css(max) = (F × X0 / Vd) × 1/(1 − e^(−Kel × tau))        Css(min) = Css(max) × e^(−Kel × tau)`,caption:`Peak and trough at steady state for an intravenous bolus multiple-dose regimen. F = bioavailability; X0 = dose; Vd = volume of distribution; Kel = elimination rate constant; tau = dosing interval.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Fluctuation = (Css(max) − Css(min)) / Css(average)        Loading dose = Maintenance dose × R = Maintenance dose / (1 − e^(−Kel × tau))`,caption:`R = accumulation ratio. When tau equals one half-life the loading dose is exactly twice the maintenance dose. Fluctuation falls as the dosing interval is shortened or as a sustained-release form is used.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Shortening tau (same total daily dose) reduces fluctuation but does not change the average steady-state concentration.`,`Doubling the daily dose doubles Css(average) in linear kinetics, but does not alter the time to reach it.`,`The principle of superposition assumes linear kinetics and no change in absorption or elimination between doses: the concentration after any dose is the sum of the concentrations that each individual dose would have produced.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Working with Michaelis–Menten drugs`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Dosing rate at steady state, R = (Vmax × Css) / (Km + Css)        rearranged:  Css = (Km × R) / (Vmax − R)`,caption:`R = daily maintenance dose rate (mg/day, corrected for bioavailability and salt factor); Vmax = maximum metabolic capacity (typically 7 mg/kg/day for phenytoin in adults); Km = Michaelis constant (typically 4 mg/L for phenytoin); Css = steady-state plasma concentration.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Phenytoin numbers`,text:`Therapeutic range 10 to 20 mg/L (mcg/mL); typical adult Km about 4 mg/L and Vmax about 7 mg/kg/day. Because Css is in the saturated region, a small dose increment (300 to 400 mg/day) can more than double the plasma level and cause nystagmus, ataxia and confusion.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Cause of non-linearity`,`Process saturated`,`Example`],rows:[[`Saturable metabolism`,`Enzyme`,`Phenytoin, ethanol, salicylate`],[`Saturable protein binding`,`Albumin sites`,`Valproate, disopyramide, naproxen`],[`Saturable absorption`,`Carrier at the gut wall`,`Riboflavin, gabapentin, amoxicillin, levodopa`],[`Saturable tubular secretion`,`Renal transporter`,`Penicillin G, ascorbic acid`],[`Auto-induction or auto-inhibition`,`Enzyme synthesis`,`Carbamazepine (induces its own metabolism)`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Half-life has no fixed meaning in non-linear kinetics`,text:`For a Michaelis–Menten drug the apparent half-life increases as the concentration rises, so quoting a single half-life is meaningless. Time to a new steady state after a dose change can therefore be far longer than the usual 4 to 5 half-lives, often 1 to 2 weeks for phenytoin.`}]},{topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material covers the linearised plots used to estimate Vmax and Km from patient data, dose adjustment strategy for saturable drugs, and the multiple-dose behaviour of oral and two-compartment drugs.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Direct linear (two-point) method:  Vmax = R1 + Km × (R1 / Css1)  with  Km = (R2 − R1) / [(R1/Css1) − (R2/Css2)]`,caption:`R1 and R2 = two different daily dose rates administered to the same patient; Css1 and Css2 = the corresponding steady-state concentrations. Solving the pair of Michaelis–Menten equations gives that patient specific Vmax and Km, which then predicts the dose needed for a target level.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A plot of R against R/Css is linear with y-intercept Vmax and slope −Km (the Michaelis–Menten equivalent of an Eadie–Hofstee plot).`,`A Lineweaver–Burk plot (1/R against 1/Css) gives y-intercept 1/Vmax and slope Km/Vmax, but weights low concentrations heavily and is less reliable clinically.`,`Phenytoin dose increments should be small: 100 mg steps below 7 mg/L, 50 mg steps between 7 and 12 mg/L, and 25 to 30 mg steps above 12 mg/L.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Time to reach 90 percent of a new steady state (Michaelis–Menten) = [Km × Vmax / (Vmax − R)^2] × [2.3 × Vmax − 0.9 × R] × Vd / Vmax`,caption:`An approximate expression showing that the time lengthens sharply as the dose rate R approaches Vmax. Km = Michaelis constant; Vmax = maximum elimination rate; R = dosing rate; Vd = volume of distribution. The practical message is that a phenytoin level should not be rechecked for at least 1 to 2 weeks after a dose change.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Multiple oral dosing and two-compartment drugs`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Css(max, oral) = [F × X0 / (Vd × (1 − e^(−Kel × tau)))] × e^(−Kel × tmax)`,caption:`Multiple-dose oral peak. F = bioavailability; X0 = dose; Vd = apparent volume of distribution; Kel = elimination rate constant; tau = dosing interval; tmax = time of peak after each dose. The trough is obtained by multiplying by e^(−Kel × (tau − tmax)).`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Change made`,`Css(average)`,`Fluctuation`,`Time to steady state`],rows:[[`Double the dose, same tau`,`Doubles`,`Increases (absolute)`,`Unchanged`],[`Halve dose, halve tau`,`Unchanged`,`Falls`,`Unchanged`],[`Give a loading dose`,`Unchanged`,`Unchanged`,`Reached immediately`],[`Enzyme induction`,`Falls`,`Increases slightly`,`Shortened`],[`Renal failure (renally cleared drug)`,`Rises`,`Falls`,`Lengthened`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Choosing tau by therapeutic index`,text:`The largest sensible dosing interval is set by the ratio of the maximum safe to the minimum effective concentration: tau(maximum) = (1/Kel) × ln (Cmax(safe) / Cmin(effective)) = 3.32 × t½ × log (Cmax/Cmin). Drugs with a narrow index need short intervals or a sustained-release form.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Salt factor and bioavailability must both be applied`,text:`Phenytoin sodium contains only 92 percent phenytoin (S = 0.92), so a 300 mg capsule delivers about 276 mg of acid. Forgetting S, or forgetting F, is the commonest arithmetic error in dose-rate calculations.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-bp-absorption-1`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The mechanism by which the majority of drugs are absorbed across the gastrointestinal membrane is`,options:[`Active transport`,`Passive diffusion`,`Pinocytosis`,`Facilitated diffusion`],answer:1,explanation:`Most drugs cross the lipid bilayer by passive diffusion down a concentration gradient: no carrier, no energy, non-saturable, first-order. Active transport is correct only for a short list of structural analogues of nutrients (levodopa, methyldopa, 5-fluorouracil), and it is saturable and energy dependent, which is not true of most drugs.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-2`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`According to the pH-partition hypothesis, the species of a drug that crosses the lipoidal membrane is the one that is`,options:[`Ionised and water soluble`,`Ionised and lipid soluble`,`Unionised and water soluble`,`Unionised and lipid soluble`],answer:3,explanation:`Brodie’s hypothesis states that only the unionised, lipid-soluble fraction partitions into the membrane. Either ionised species is ruled out because the charge is repelled by the hydrophobic core; an unionised but purely water-soluble molecule has too low a partition coefficient to enter the lipid phase.`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-3`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A weak acid has a pKa of 4.4. What percentage of it is present in the unionised (absorbable) form in gastric fluid of pH 1.4?`,options:[`9.1 percent`,`50 percent`,`99.9 percent`,`0.1 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`For an acid, log([ionised]/[unionised]) = pH − pKa = 1.4 − 4.4 = −3, so [ionised]/[unionised] = 10^−3 = 1/1000. Unionised fraction = 1000/(1000 + 1) = 0.999, that is 99.9 percent. The 0.1 percent answer is what you get if you reverse the ratio, and 9.1 percent is the answer for a base one pH unit from its pKa.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-4`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A weak base has a pKa of 8.4. What percentage of it is unionised in plasma at pH 7.4?`,options:[`90.9 percent`,`9.1 percent`,`50 percent`,`99 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`Percent unionised base = 100/(1 + 10^(pKa − pH)) = 100/(1 + 10^1) = 100/11 = 9.1 percent. The tempting 90.9 percent is the ionised fraction: for a base, a pH below the pKa means most of the drug is protonated and therefore ionised.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-5`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following would increase the rate of gastric emptying and therefore hasten the onset of action of an orally administered drug?`,options:[`A fatty meal`,`Atropine given with the drug`,`A large volume of cold water on an empty stomach`,`A hypertonic saline solution`],answer:2,explanation:`A large volume of cold, essentially isotonic fluid distends the stomach and empties rapidly. Fats are the strongest physiological brake on gastric emptying, hypertonic solutions are held back by duodenal osmoreceptors, and anticholinergics such as atropine relax gastric smooth muscle and delay emptying.`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-6`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug with low aqueous solubility but high intestinal permeability belongs to which BCS class, and what is its rate-limiting step?`,options:[`Class I, gastric emptying`,`Class II, dissolution`,`Class III, permeation`,`Class IV, both dissolution and permeation`],answer:1,explanation:`Low solubility with high permeability defines Class II, where dissolution in the gut fluid is the bottleneck; these are the drugs that respond to micronisation, salt formation and solid dispersion. Class III has the opposite profile (high solubility, low permeability) and is limited by membrane transport, not by dissolution.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-7`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Aspirin (pKa 3.5) is more than 99 percent ionised in the small intestine yet most of an oral dose is absorbed there rather than from the stomach. The best explanation is`,options:[`The intestinal microclimate pH is more acidic than the stomach`,`Aspirin is actively transported by PEPT1 in the jejunum`,`The huge absorptive surface area and long residence time of the intestine outweigh the lower unionised fraction`,`Ionised aspirin diffuses through aqueous pores in the intestine`],answer:2,explanation:`Absorption rate is proportional to surface area as well as to the unionised fraction. The small intestine offers roughly 200 square metres and several hours of contact against a few hundred square centimetres and 30 minutes in the stomach, which more than compensates for the 1000-fold fall in unionised fraction. The microclimate pH (about 5.3) is lower than the luminal pH but still far above the gastric pH, so option (a) is wrong.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-absorption-8`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For a weak acid of pKa 4.4 at equilibrium between gastric fluid (pH 1.4) and plasma (pH 7.4), the theoretical ratio of total drug concentration in plasma to that in gastric fluid is approximately`,options:[`1 : 1000`,`1000 : 1`,`1 : 100`,`100 : 1`],answer:1,explanation:`Only the unionised form equilibrates across the membrane, so total concentration on each side is proportional to (1 + 10^(pH − pKa)). Plasma gives 1 + 10^3 = 1001; gastric fluid gives 1 + 10^(−3) = 1.001. The ratio is about 1000 : 1 in favour of plasma, the classic ion-trapping result that explains why a weak acid does not diffuse back out of the blood into the stomach.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-1`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Plasma proteins that bind basic drugs such as lignocaine and propranolol most avidly are`,options:[`Albumin`,`Alpha-1-acid glycoprotein`,`Beta-globulin`,`High density lipoprotein`],answer:1,explanation:`Alpha-1-acid glycoprotein (orosomucoid) is the principal carrier of basic drugs; albumin, being itself an anionic protein at plasma pH, preferentially binds acidic and neutral drugs such as warfarin, phenytoin and salicylates. Lipoproteins carry only very lipophilic molecules such as cyclosporine.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-2`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A drug with an apparent volume of distribution of about 3 L in a 70 kg adult is most likely`,options:[`Highly bound to plasma proteins and confined to plasma`,`Concentrated in adipose tissue`,`Distributed through total body water`,`Extensively bound to tissue proteins`],answer:0,explanation:`Plasma volume is about 3 L, so a Vd of that size means the drug essentially never leaves the vascular space, which is typical of heavily albumin-bound drugs such as warfarin and of large molecules such as heparin. Fat accumulation or tissue binding would give a Vd far larger than total body water (42 L), not smaller.`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-3`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 500 mg intravenous bolus gives an extrapolated zero-time plasma concentration of 25 µg/mL. The apparent volume of distribution is`,options:[`2 L`,`12.5 L`,`20 L`,`50 L`],answer:2,explanation:`Vd = Dose / C0. Convert to consistent units: 25 µg/mL = 25 mg/L. Vd = 500 mg ÷ 25 mg/L = 20 L. The common error is to treat 25 µg/mL as 25 mg/L only after dividing wrongly by 1000, which produces the 20000 L or 0.02 L type of answer.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-4`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug has a clearance of 3.465 L/h and an elimination half-life of 6 hours. Its apparent volume of distribution is`,options:[`20.8 L`,`30 L`,`2.4 L`,`60 L`],answer:1,explanation:`Kel = 0.693/t½ = 0.693/6 = 0.1155 per hour. Vd = Cl/Kel = 3.465 ÷ 0.1155 = 30 L. Multiplying clearance by half-life instead of dividing by Kel gives 20.8 L, which is the usual trap; remember Cl = Kel × Vd, so Vd = Cl/Kel.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-5`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug is normally 98 percent bound to albumin. A displacing agent reduces the binding to 96 percent. The free (active) concentration`,options:[`Increases by 2 percent`,`Doubles`,`Halves`,`Is unchanged`],answer:1,explanation:`Free fraction rises from 2 percent to 4 percent, so the unbound concentration doubles, a 100 percent increase, even though total binding changed by only 2 percentage points. This amplification is why displacement matters only for drugs bound more than about 90 percent and with a narrow therapeutic index.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-6`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Administration of a sulphonamide to a neonate can precipitate kernicterus because the sulphonamide`,options:[`Inhibits glucuronyl transferase`,`Displaces bilirubin from albumin binding sites`,`Increases the permeability of the blood–brain barrier to bilirubin`,`Causes haemolysis, raising bilirubin production`],answer:1,explanation:`Sulphonamides compete for the same albumin sites as bilirubin; the displaced free bilirubin crosses the immature blood–brain barrier and deposits in the basal ganglia. Chloramphenicol, not sulphonamide, is the classic glucuronidation problem in neonates (grey baby syndrome), and sulphonamide-induced haemolysis needs G6PD deficiency and is not the mechanism of kernicterus.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-7`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Using Vd = Vp + Vt × (fu / fu,t), which change will increase the apparent volume of distribution?`,options:[`A fall in the fraction unbound in plasma`,`A rise in the fraction unbound in plasma`,`A rise in the fraction unbound in tissue`,`An increase in plasma volume only`],answer:1,explanation:`Vd is directly proportional to fu and inversely proportional to fu,t. Displacement from plasma proteins raises fu, drives drug into the tissues and enlarges Vd. Raising fu,t means the drug is less tightly held in tissue, so it returns to plasma and Vd falls, which is exactly what quinidine does to digoxin.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-distribution-protein-binding-8`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a Scatchard plot for drug–protein binding, r/[Df] is plotted against r. The slope and the x-axis intercept respectively give`,options:[`Ka and n`,`−Ka and n`,`n and Ka`,`−n and Ka`],answer:1,explanation:`Rearranging the Langmuir isotherm gives r/[Df] = nKa − rKa, an equation of a straight line with slope −Ka and intercept on the r axis equal to n, the number of binding sites per protein molecule. The y-intercept is nKa. A curved (biphasic) Scatchard plot indicates more than one class of binding site.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-1`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is a phase II (conjugation) reaction?`,options:[`N-dealkylation`,`Aromatic hydroxylation`,`Glucuronidation`,`Azo reduction`],answer:2,explanation:`Glucuronidation, catalysed by UDP-glucuronosyl transferase, is the commonest phase II route in humans and produces a highly water-soluble, usually inactive conjugate. Dealkylation, hydroxylation and reduction are all phase I functionalisation reactions that only introduce or expose a polar group.`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-2`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Probenecid prolongs the action of penicillin mainly by`,options:[`Inhibiting hepatic metabolism of penicillin`,`Competitively inhibiting active tubular secretion of penicillin`,`Increasing plasma protein binding of penicillin`,`Alkalinising the urine`],answer:1,explanation:`Penicillin is cleared largely by the organic anion transporter in the proximal tubule; probenecid, an organic acid, competes for the same carrier and cuts renal clearance, raising and prolonging penicillin levels. Penicillin undergoes very little hepatic metabolism, so option (a) cannot be the mechanism.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-3`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Hepatic blood flow is 1.5 L/min and a drug has a hepatic extraction ratio of 0.8. Its hepatic clearance and its maximum possible oral bioavailability are`,options:[`1.2 L/min and 20 percent`,`0.3 L/min and 80 percent`,`1.2 L/min and 80 percent`,`1.875 L/min and 20 percent`],answer:0,explanation:`Cl(hepatic) = Q × ER = 1.5 × 0.8 = 1.2 L/min. F(max) = 1 − ER = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2, that is 20 percent, assuming complete absorption from the gut. Option (c) mixes the correct clearance with the fraction extracted rather than the fraction escaping extraction.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-4`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`For a 60-year-old male weighing 72 kg with a serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL, the creatinine clearance by the Cockcroft–Gault equation is approximately`,options:[`52 mL/min`,`68 mL/min`,`80 mL/min`,`120 mL/min`],answer:2,explanation:`CrCl = [(140 − 60) × 72] / (72 × 1.0) = (80 × 72)/72 = 80 mL/min. No 0.85 correction is applied because the patient is male. Choosing 68 mL/min corresponds to wrongly applying the female factor (80 × 0.85 = 68).`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-5`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Urine is collected for 4 hours; 60 mg of unchanged drug is recovered while the average plasma concentration is 5 mg/L. The renal clearance is`,options:[`3 L/h`,`12 L/h`,`0.25 L/h`,`300 L/h`],answer:0,explanation:`Rate of urinary excretion = 60 mg ÷ 4 h = 15 mg/h. Cl(renal) = rate of excretion ÷ plasma concentration = 15 mg/h ÷ 5 mg/L = 3 L/h (50 mL/min). Dividing the total amount rather than the rate by the concentration gives the incorrect 12 L/h.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-6`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug is 50 percent bound to plasma proteins and glomerular filtration rate is 120 mL/min. A measured renal clearance of 30 mL/min indicates`,options:[`Filtration only`,`Net tubular secretion`,`Net tubular reabsorption`,`Complete extraction by the kidney`],answer:2,explanation:`Expected filtration clearance = fu × GFR = 0.5 × 120 = 60 mL/min. The observed 30 mL/min is lower, so part of the filtered drug is being reabsorbed from the tubule. A value above 60 mL/min would have proved active secretion, and a value of exactly 60 mL/min would indicate filtration alone.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-7`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Enzyme induction by rifampicin will have the greatest effect on the oral bioavailability of`,options:[`Warfarin (extraction ratio 0.05)`,`Theophylline (extraction ratio 0.1)`,`Propranolol (extraction ratio 0.8)`,`Gentamicin (renally cleared)`],answer:2,explanation:`For a high extraction ratio drug, systemic clearance is limited by liver blood flow and changes little with induction, but the first-pass loss (1 − F) rises sharply, so oral bioavailability collapses. For low extraction drugs like warfarin and theophylline induction raises systemic clearance and lowers plasma levels but barely alters F; gentamicin is not metabolised at all.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-metabolism-excretion-8`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A broad-spectrum antibiotic can cause failure of a low-dose oral contraceptive because it`,options:[`Induces CYP3A4 metabolism of ethinyl oestradiol`,`Suppresses gut flora and interrupts enterohepatic recycling of the oestrogen conjugate`,`Displaces the oestrogen from albumin`,`Increases the biliary excretion of the oestrogen`],answer:1,explanation:`Ethinyl oestradiol is glucuronidated, excreted in bile, then hydrolysed back to active drug by bacterial beta-glucuronidase and reabsorbed. Killing the flora breaks that recycling loop and lowers systemic exposure. Enzyme induction is a real cause of contraceptive failure, but with rifampicin and anticonvulsants, not with broad-spectrum antibiotics in general.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-1`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A drug follows first-order elimination with Kel of 0.0693 per hour. Its half-life is`,options:[`6.93 hours`,`10 hours`,`14.4 hours`,`0.048 hours`],answer:1,explanation:`t½ = 0.693 / Kel = 0.693 ÷ 0.0693 = 10 hours. The 14.4 hour answer comes from using 1/Kel, which is the mean residence time for a one-compartment intravenous bolus, not the half-life.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-2`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In a one-compartment open model after an intravenous bolus, a plot of log plasma concentration against time is linear with a slope equal to`,options:[`−Kel`,`−Kel / 2.303`,`−2.303 × Kel`,`−0.693 / Kel`],answer:1,explanation:`Taking common logarithms of C = C0 e^(−Kel t) gives log C = log C0 − Kel t / 2.303, so the slope is −Kel/2.303 and Kel = −2.303 × slope. The slope is −Kel only if natural logarithm (ln C) is plotted, which is the standard distractor here.`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-3`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Plasma concentration falls from 20 mg/L at 2 hours to 5 mg/L at 6 hours after an intravenous bolus. The elimination rate constant is`,options:[`0.347 per hour`,`0.173 per hour`,`0.693 per hour`,`0.231 per hour`],answer:0,explanation:`Kel = ln(C1/C2) / (t2 − t1) = ln(20/5) / (6 − 2) = ln 4 / 4 = 1.386 ÷ 4 = 0.3466 per hour. Note the concentration halved twice in 4 hours, so t½ = 2 hours and Kel = 0.693/2 = 0.347 per hour, confirming the result. 0.173 per hour would correspond to a 4-hour half-life.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-4`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 400 mg intravenous bolus gives C0 = 20 mg/L and a half-life of 4 hours. The AUC from zero to infinity and the total clearance are`,options:[`80 mg·h/L and 5 L/h`,`115.4 mg·h/L and 3.47 L/h`,`115.4 mg·h/L and 5 L/h`,`80 mg·h/L and 3.47 L/h`],answer:1,explanation:`Kel = 0.693/4 = 0.17325 per hour. AUC = C0/Kel = 20 ÷ 0.17325 = 115.4 mg·h/L. Cl = Dose/AUC = 400 ÷ 115.4 = 3.47 L/h, which also equals Kel × Vd = 0.17325 × 20 L. Multiplying C0 by t½ instead of dividing by Kel gives the incorrect 80 mg·h/L.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-5`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug with clearance 4 L/h and volume of distribution 40 L is infused intravenously at 20 mg/h. The steady-state plasma concentration and the loading dose needed to reach it at once are`,options:[`5 mg/L and 200 mg`,`0.5 mg/L and 20 mg`,`5 mg/L and 20 mg`,`80 mg/L and 3200 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`Css = R0/Cl = 20 mg/h ÷ 4 L/h = 5 mg/L. Loading dose = Css × Vd = 5 mg/L × 40 L = 200 mg. Note the loading dose depends only on Vd and the maintenance rate only on clearance; using clearance in the loading dose calculation gives the wrong 20 mg.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-6`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Plasma concentrations are 4 mg/L at 2 hours and 6 mg/L at 4 hours. The AUC over this interval by the trapezoidal rule is`,options:[`5 mg·h/L`,`10 mg·h/L`,`20 mg·h/L`,`2.5 mg·h/L`],answer:1,explanation:`AUC = (C1 + C2) × (t2 − t1)/2 = (4 + 6) × 2 / 2 = 10 mg·h/L. Forgetting to divide by 2 gives 20 mg·h/L, and averaging the concentrations without multiplying by the time interval gives 5 mg·h/L.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-7`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The Wagner–Nelson method is used to determine the absorption rate constant. Its principal limitation is that it`,options:[`Requires urinary excretion data in addition to plasma data`,`Is valid only for a drug that obeys one-compartment kinetics`,`Cannot be used when absorption is first order`,`Requires intravenous data in the same subject`],answer:1,explanation:`Wagner–Nelson assumes a one-compartment model, needing only oral plasma data and the terminal Kel; it fails when a distribution phase is present. The method that handles a two-compartment drug is Loo–Riegelman, and that is the one requiring intravenous data in the same subject, which is why option (d) is the tempting but wrong choice.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-compartment-models-8`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For an oral product showing flip-flop kinetics, the terminal slope of the log concentration versus time plot gives`,options:[`The elimination rate constant Kel`,`The absorption rate constant Ka`,`The hybrid constant beta`,`The sum of Ka and Kel`],answer:1,explanation:`Flip-flop kinetics occur when Ka is smaller than Kel, typically with sustained-release or depot products; the slower of the two processes always controls the terminal phase, so here it is absorption that dictates the terminal slope. In the normal situation Ka is much greater than Kel and the terminal slope legitimately gives Kel.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-1`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The pharmacokinetic parameter that reflects the extent of drug absorption is`,options:[`Cmax`,`tmax`,`AUC`,`Elimination half-life`],answer:2,explanation:`AUC is proportional to the total amount of drug that reaches the systemic circulation, so it measures extent. Cmax and tmax are indices of the rate of absorption; half-life is a property of elimination and distribution and is unaffected by how much drug was absorbed.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-2`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Two products containing the same drug in the same dose and dosage form, meeting the same compendial standards, are termed`,options:[`Bioequivalent products`,`Pharmaceutical equivalents`,`Therapeutic equivalents`,`Pharmaceutical alternatives`],answer:1,explanation:`Pharmaceutical equivalence is a purely chemical and dosage-form criterion; it says nothing about absorption. Bioequivalence additionally requires that the rate and extent of absorption are statistically indistinguishable, and pharmaceutical alternatives contain the same moiety but as a different salt, ester or dosage form.`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-3`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 500 mg oral dose gives an AUC of 60 mg·h/L and a 100 mg intravenous dose gives an AUC of 30 mg·h/L. The absolute bioavailability of the oral product is`,options:[`20 percent`,`40 percent`,`50 percent`,`200 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`F = (AUC oral / AUC iv) × (Dose iv / Dose oral) = (60/30) × (100/500) = 2 × 0.2 = 0.4, that is 40 percent. Forgetting the dose correction gives the impossible 200 percent, which is the commonest error in this calculation.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-4`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A test tablet (250 mg) gives an AUC of 45 mg·h/L and the reference tablet (500 mg) gives 75 mg·h/L. The relative bioavailability of the test product is`,options:[`60 percent`,`90 percent`,`120 percent`,`150 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`Fr = (AUC test / AUC reference) × (Dose reference / Dose test) = (45/75) × (500/250) = 0.6 × 2 = 1.2, that is 120 percent. Relative bioavailability may legitimately exceed 100 percent; omitting the dose correction would give the wrong 60 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-5`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Two formulations are declared bioequivalent when`,options:[`The 95 percent confidence interval of the test/reference ratio lies within 80 to 120 percent`,`The 90 percent confidence interval of the log-transformed AUC and Cmax ratio lies within 80.00 to 125.00 percent`,`The mean AUC values differ by less than 20 percent`,`The dissolution profiles have an f2 value above 50`],answer:1,explanation:`The regulatory criterion is a 90 percent confidence interval (two one-sided tests at alpha 0.05) on the geometric mean ratio of log-transformed AUC and Cmax, falling wholly within 80.00 to 125.00 percent. The limits are 80 to 125, not 80 to 120, because they are reciprocal on the log scale; comparing means alone ignores variability, and f2 is a dissolution criterion, not a bioequivalence one.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-6`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a standard two-period crossover bioequivalence study the washout period should be at least`,options:[`Twice the tmax`,`5 elimination half-lives`,`24 hours`,`3 times the dosing interval`],answer:1,explanation:`Five half-lives removes about 97 percent of the first dose, so no measurable drug carries over into the second period. A fixed 24-hour interval is arbitrary and would be far too short for a drug with a long half-life; tmax describes absorption and is irrelevant to the elimination of residual drug.`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-7`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A level A in vitro in vivo correlation is defined as`,options:[`A correlation between mean dissolution time and mean residence time`,`A single-point relationship between percentage dissolved and Cmax`,`A point-to-point relationship between the in vitro dissolution curve and the in vivo absorption curve`,`A correlation between dissolution rate constant and elimination rate constant`],answer:2,explanation:`Level A is a point-to-point (usually linear) relationship between the whole in vitro dissolution profile and the whole in vivo input profile, and it is the only level that can justify a dissolution-based biowaiver for a modified-release product. The statistical-moment comparison described in option (a) is level B, and the single-point comparison in option (b) is level C.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-bioavailability-be-8`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a crossover bioequivalence study, a statistically significant sequence effect in the ANOVA most likely indicates`,options:[`That the products are not bioequivalent`,`Inadequate washout with carry-over of drug`,`A period effect caused by seasonal change`,`That the sample size was too large`],answer:1,explanation:`A sequence (carry-over) effect means the response in period 2 depends on what was given in period 1, which almost always reflects an insufficient washout; the crossover analysis is then invalid and only the first-period data can be used, as a parallel study. A sequence effect is a design failure and says nothing directly about whether the products are bioequivalent.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-1`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which drug is the classic example of capacity-limited (Michaelis–Menten) kinetics within its therapeutic range?`,options:[`Paracetamol`,`Phenytoin`,`Amoxicillin`,`Atenolol`],answer:1,explanation:`Phenytoin saturates its metabolising enzymes at plasma levels of 10 to 20 mg/L, inside the therapeutic window, so small dose increases produce disproportionate rises in concentration. Paracetamol shows saturation only in overdose, while amoxicillin and atenolol follow linear kinetics at ordinary doses.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-2`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`For a drug given repeatedly at a fixed dose and interval, steady state is reached in approximately`,options:[`1 to 2 half-lives`,`4 to 5 half-lives`,`10 half-lives`,`A time that depends on the size of the dose`],answer:1,explanation:`After 4 half-lives 93.75 percent and after 5 half-lives about 97 percent of steady state is achieved, and this is independent of dose size and dosing interval. Increasing the dose raises the plateau but does not shorten the time taken to reach it; only a loading dose does that.`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-3`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug with a half-life of 8 hours is given every 8 hours. The accumulation ratio at steady state is`,options:[`1.0`,`1.5`,`2.0`,`4.0`],answer:2,explanation:`R = 1/(1 − e^(−Kel × tau)). With tau equal to one half-life, e^(−Kel×tau) = 0.5, so R = 1/(1 − 0.5) = 2. Concentrations at steady state are therefore twice those seen after the first dose, and a loading dose of twice the maintenance dose reaches steady state immediately.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-4`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug of clearance 5 L/h and bioavailability 0.8 is given as 250 mg every 8 hours. The average steady-state plasma concentration is`,options:[`5.0 mg/L`,`6.25 mg/L`,`2.5 mg/L`,`40 mg/L`],answer:0,explanation:`Css(average) = (F × X0)/(Cl × tau) = (0.8 × 250)/(5 × 8) = 200/40 = 5.0 mg/L. Ignoring bioavailability gives 6.25 mg/L, which is the standard distractor; note that Css(average) depends on clearance, never on volume of distribution.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-5`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient needs a phenytoin steady-state level of 15 mg/L. If Vmax is 500 mg/day and Km is 4 mg/L, the required daily dose (assuming F and salt factor of 1) is about`,options:[`133 mg/day`,`250 mg/day`,`395 mg/day`,`625 mg/day`],answer:2,explanation:`R = (Vmax × Css)/(Km + Css) = (500 × 15)/(4 + 15) = 7500/19 = 394.7, about 395 mg/day. Note how close this is to Vmax: raising the dose to 450 mg/day would demand a Css of (4 × 450)/(500 − 450) = 36 mg/L, well into the toxic range, which is why phenytoin is titrated in small steps.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-6`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`If the total daily dose is kept the same but the dosing interval is halved (half the dose given twice as often), which of the following changes?`,options:[`Average steady-state concentration rises`,`Time to reach steady state shortens`,`Peak-to-trough fluctuation decreases`,`Clearance decreases`],answer:2,explanation:`Css(average) = F × X0 / (Cl × tau); halving both X0 and tau leaves the ratio and therefore the average concentration unchanged, but the peaks are lower and the troughs higher, so fluctuation falls. Time to steady state depends only on half-life, and clearance is a property of the patient, not of the schedule.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-7`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which set of observations, on doubling the dose of a drug, indicates non-linear pharmacokinetics due to saturable metabolism?`,options:[`AUC doubles, half-life unchanged, clearance unchanged`,`AUC more than doubles, half-life increases, clearance decreases`,`AUC less than doubles, half-life decreases, clearance increases`,`AUC doubles, half-life increases, clearance decreases`],answer:1,explanation:`When metabolising enzymes saturate, the eliminating capacity cannot keep pace with the dose, so clearance falls, apparent half-life lengthens and AUC rises more than proportionally. Option (a) is textbook linear kinetics, and option (c) describes the opposite pattern seen with auto-induction, as with carbamazepine on repeated dosing.`,tag:`Concept`},{id:`q-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-8`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug has a half-life of 6 hours and is given every 6 hours as an intravenous bolus of 100 mg into a volume of distribution of 25 L. The steady-state peak and trough concentrations are`,options:[`4 and 2 mg/L`,`8 and 4 mg/L`,`2 and 1 mg/L`,`8 and 2 mg/L`],answer:1,explanation:`Concentration rise per dose = 100 mg ÷ 25 L = 4 mg/L. With tau equal to one half-life the accumulation ratio is 1/(1 − 0.5) = 2, so Css(max) = 4 × 2 = 8 mg/L and Css(min) = 8 × 0.5 = 4 mg/L. Answer (a) is the first-dose peak and trough, before accumulation has occurred.`,tag:`Numerical`}],n=[{id:`f-bp-absorption-1`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,front:`Predominant mechanism of drug absorption?`,back:`Passive diffusion: no carrier, no energy, non-saturable, first order.`},{id:`f-bp-absorption-2`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,front:`Percent unionised of a weak acid, formula?`,back:`% unionised = 100 / (1 + 10^(pH − pKa)).`},{id:`f-bp-absorption-3`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,front:`Percent unionised of a weak base, formula?`,back:`% unionised = 100 / (1 + 10^(pKa − pH)).`},{id:`f-bp-absorption-4`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,front:`BCS Class II profile and rate-limiting step?`,back:`Low solubility, high permeability; dissolution is rate limiting (ketoconazole, glibenclamide).`},{id:`f-bp-absorption-5`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,front:`Noyes–Whitney equation?`,back:`dC/dt = (D × A / h) × (Cs − C); increase A by micronisation, increase Cs by salt or amorphous form.`},{id:`f-bp-absorption-6`,topicId:`bp-absorption`,front:`Rate-limiting physiological step for oral onset of action?`,back:`Gastric emptying, because absorption occurs mainly in the small intestine.`},{id:`f-bp-distribution-protein-binding-1`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,front:`Apparent volume of distribution formula?`,back:`Vd = Dose / C0 = Cl / Kel. Units L or L/kg.`},{id:`f-bp-distribution-protein-binding-2`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,front:`Which plasma protein binds acidic drugs? Basic drugs?`,back:`Acidic to albumin; basic to alpha-1-acid glycoprotein.`},{id:`f-bp-distribution-protein-binding-3`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,front:`Plasma, ECF and total body water volumes in a 70 kg adult?`,back:`Plasma about 3 L, extracellular fluid about 14 L, total body water about 42 L (0.6 L/kg).`},{id:`f-bp-distribution-protein-binding-4`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,front:`Binding falls from 98 to 96 percent. Effect on free drug?`,back:`Free fraction rises from 2 to 4 percent: the active concentration doubles.`},{id:`f-bp-distribution-protein-binding-5`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,front:`Physiological equation for Vd?`,back:`Vd = Vp + Vt × (fu / fu,t). Vd rises when plasma binding falls, falls when tissue binding falls.`},{id:`f-bp-distribution-protein-binding-6`,topicId:`bp-distribution-protein-binding`,front:`Sulphonamide plus neonate equals?`,back:`Displacement of bilirubin from albumin, causing kernicterus.`},{id:`f-bp-metabolism-excretion-1`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,front:`Hepatic clearance in terms of blood flow?`,back:`Cl(H) = Q × ER, where ER = (Ca − Cv)/Ca; maximum oral F = 1 − ER.`},{id:`f-bp-metabolism-excretion-2`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,front:`Renal clearance formula from urine data?`,back:`Cl(R) = (Cu × Vu) / Cp = rate of urinary excretion divided by plasma concentration.`},{id:`f-bp-metabolism-excretion-3`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,front:`Cockcroft–Gault equation?`,back:`CrCl = [(140 − age) × weight in kg] / (72 × serum creatinine in mg/dL), times 0.85 for females.`},{id:`f-bp-metabolism-excretion-4`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,front:`How do you prove net tubular secretion?`,back:`Renal clearance greater than fu × GFR (for example penicillin, PAH).`},{id:`f-bp-metabolism-excretion-5`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,front:`Three high extraction ratio drugs?`,back:`Propranolol, lignocaine, morphine (also verapamil, nitroglycerin): low and variable oral bioavailability.`},{id:`f-bp-metabolism-excretion-6`,topicId:`bp-metabolism-excretion`,front:`What is enterohepatic recycling?`,back:`Biliary excretion of a conjugate, hydrolysis by gut beta-glucuronidase, reabsorption; prolongs half-life (oral contraceptives, morphine, rifampicin).`},{id:`f-bp-compartment-models-1`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,front:`First-order half-life equation?`,back:`t½ = 0.693 / Kel.`},{id:`f-bp-compartment-models-2`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,front:`Relationship between clearance, Kel and Vd?`,back:`Cl = Kel × Vd.`},{id:`f-bp-compartment-models-3`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,front:`AUC after an intravenous bolus?`,back:`AUC(0 to infinity) = C0 / Kel = Dose / Cl.`},{id:`f-bp-compartment-models-4`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,front:`Steady-state concentration during infusion, and the loading dose?`,back:`Css = R0 / Cl; loading dose = Css × Vd.`},{id:`f-bp-compartment-models-5`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,front:`Trapezoidal rule for AUC between two points?`,back:`AUC = (C1 + C2) × (t2 − t1) / 2; add Clast/Kel for the tail.`},{id:`f-bp-compartment-models-6`,topicId:`bp-compartment-models`,front:`What does the method of residuals give for oral data?`,back:`The residual (feathered) line has slope −Ka/2.303, giving the absorption rate constant.`},{id:`f-bp-bioavailability-be-1`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,front:`Absolute bioavailability formula?`,back:`F = (AUC oral / AUC iv) × (Dose iv / Dose oral).`},{id:`f-bp-bioavailability-be-2`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,front:`Relative bioavailability formula?`,back:`Fr = (AUC test / AUC standard) × (Dose standard / Dose test); can exceed 100 percent.`},{id:`f-bp-bioavailability-be-3`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,front:`Bioequivalence acceptance criterion?`,back:`90 percent confidence interval of log-transformed AUC and Cmax ratio within 80.00 to 125.00 percent.`},{id:`f-bp-bioavailability-be-4`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,front:`Which parameters measure rate, and which measures extent, of absorption?`,back:`Rate: Cmax and tmax. Extent: AUC.`},{id:`f-bp-bioavailability-be-5`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,front:`Level A IVIVC?`,back:`Point-to-point correlation of the in vitro dissolution curve with the in vivo absorption curve; supports a biowaiver.`},{id:`f-bp-bioavailability-be-6`,topicId:`bp-bioavailability-be`,front:`Standard bioequivalence study design?`,back:`Single dose, two-way randomised crossover in 18 to 36 fasted healthy volunteers, washout of at least 5 half-lives.`},{id:`f-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-1`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,front:`Michaelis–Menten elimination equation?`,back:`−dC/dt = (Vmax × C)/(Km + C); first order when C is much less than Km, zero order when C is much greater than Km.`},{id:`f-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-2`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,front:`Phenytoin dosing rate at steady state?`,back:`R = (Vmax × Css)/(Km + Css); typical adult Km 4 mg/L, Vmax 7 mg/kg/day.`},{id:`f-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-3`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,front:`Accumulation ratio formula?`,back:`R = 1/(1 − e^(−Kel × tau)); equals 2 when tau equals one half-life.`},{id:`f-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-4`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,front:`Average steady-state concentration on multiple dosing?`,back:`Css(average) = (F × X0)/(Cl × tau).`},{id:`f-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-5`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,front:`Effect of halving dose and halving interval?`,back:`Css(average) unchanged, fluctuation reduced, time to steady state unchanged.`},{id:`f-bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing-6`,topicId:`bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing`,front:`Loading dose from maintenance dose?`,back:`Loading dose = maintenance dose × accumulation ratio = maintenance dose / (1 − e^(−Kel × tau)).`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};